Chapter One, as Mark drafted it.
)))
Chapter One

A low-caste, runt of a vampire is looked down upon, for many reasons.  One is their lesser power, skill, and experience.  Another reason is the ruthless hierarchy within which coven society runs; the weak are mistreated, insulted, even brutalized.  The most pernicious reason, though, is the belief—well-founded or not—that they did not fully give in to their true nature, their bloodlust: too weak-spirited to sate it.  The complete opposite is the Blood King: the leader of a coven, he is the strongest, his will is supreme, he has no mercy when his bloodlust strikes.  He is, above all, cruel.  He bears no fondness for the weak, often using them as pawns for the Hunters, in case they got the better of the coven in a skirmish, or even out of sick pleasure.  “Daywalkers,” he and the coven called these not-quite-vampires in mockery.

A Daywalker was on the prowl outside his coven, under the light of the sun.  Though it stung, he could hunt in full sunlight without burning up… only he would walk unseen, undetectable except by sound and touch.  The pain was tolerable if it did not last too long, and it gave him a predatory advantage.  Even better, this way he was alone.  He had thoughts unlike other vampires, but vampires don’t share with each other pointless, childish thoughts.  Like blood types, for example: he was so sure different people had different blood types, as most made him sick and weak.  He figured his own blood type must be rare, and that it was not compatible with the blood of most.  In fact, he preferred to feed on animals, as he didn’t have to worry about the sickness, though it still left him weak.  He was not a proper vampire, by any means, nor had he the full powers of one, honed by lifetimes of sharpening his skills, powers, experience, despite having traversed the length of more than a few lives of men.  This left him in a predictable situation with his coven: he was at the bottom of the hierarchy, practically an outcast.  He had the smallest cove, no respect, many beatings, and no friends, if they even existed in any vampire culture. His only redeeming quality was his extreme resistance to hypnosis—not even his Blood King could hypnotize him... well, that, and his ability to avoid the others.  He hated the other vampires, he despised himself, and he hated the Path of Air, with their draconian Order forever hunting his kind, intent on ridding the world of all vampires.

“What a hot day,” he said to himself, at noon in the middle of summer.  He was walking on a path bordered by trees on either side, but no shade along the path.  It was better this way.  If he were to go into the shade, he might start to show ever so little, even if only giving off a scent, potentially thwarting his hunt.  There were no worthwhile animals nearby, right now, but he heard a group of children starting to run down the path toward him, playing some carefree game.  Insipid brats: playing in the foothills of a small mountain filled to the brim with vampires that would drain them dry in seconds.  Infuriated at their ignorance, their naked… inanity, he crept into their midst, unseen, and from the middle of their troupe let out a bloodcurdling scream.  They panicked, screaming as they ran home.  For the tiniest of moments he let loose a mirthless laugh, though he was immediately fell ensconced in a placid nothingness of emotion.  This quickly kindled into a dry anger.  “Stupid. Stupid. Stupid.  This is why the Red Order hunts us.  Parents allow their children to play wherever, and don’t tell them where it is clearly not safe to be, let alone play.  Some members of the coven would travel leagues to find the sweetest blood, but a human in a coven’s backyard is assured to become prey,” he said aloud.

He frequently talked to himself, as if to a companion, though he was under no illusion that there was another party present.  He was quite comfortable with this by now, one of his few comforts.  While the others only called him Daywalker, he called himself Ixë, which in his past tongue meant Echo, or Sound, referring to his voice; he couldn’t remember his original name.  “Did I use to play like those brats?” He asked himself.   It had been too long to recall so many things.  “Things I had once taken for granted,” he said.  He hated this, too, or maybe he was indifferent: it was hard for him to tell.  Would he give up this life, even if he could?  “It doesn’t matter anyways,” he said.  Owned by his inalienable fate: inhuman, immortal, invisible in the light, this bland life continues.  Not only could he hardly remember his former human life, he no longer felt any sense of novelty.  Every day was exactly the same, even if something different occurred.  His singular overwhelming memory was of his First Blood, never to be matched ever again.  Every day that followed, followed the previous day, infinitely, until it reached that fateful day.  Every day ensued an endless repetition of dreamless, mediocre monotony, on the verge of madness and hollow displays of power.

He spotted a rabbit, peeking out of a bush nearby, and locking gazes with it, lulled it into a stupor, whereupon he deftly scooped it up, wrung its neck, and fed.  Still weak but now satisfied, he turned to the small mountain, hoping to reach his cove without meeting anyone.

After passing the den unaccosted and finally settling into his cove, he heard a clamor: running, screaming, sounds of death.  “A raid!” He exclaimed, fetching his most valuable possessions: his small mace, his bow and arrows, and his book on spirokinesis—all of which he had pilfered off the undead corpses of fallen vampires—and prepared to run outside.  He just needed to get through the den and out into the sunlight.  Expecting to see red cloaks, he was shocked to find in the den a young man, wearing a grey robe and blue cloak, bursting through the labyrinthian  coven, wielding a light in his hand of pure starlight, yet somehow brighter than the sun.  But he had no signs of cuts or blood on him: he was not of the Path of Air… and those dung beetles wore white and red, playing with trifling attempts of hypnosis and spirokinesis, hardly able to challenge a coven rearing its full strength, each denizen wielding decades’ to centuries’ experience in killing, in their own stronghold.   So the red cloaks used strategies of overwhelming numbers, bursting in unpredictably at inconvenient times of the day like this, hoping to catch them unprepared.  This young man in blue, however—by a quick glance almost a boy, short, with brown hair that whipped around with his cat-like swiftness—systematically experienced unchallenged victory after victory, hunting down an entire petty kingdom that thrashed about in desperation and futile attempts.  A large group of red cloaks would have struggled after a hour or so to stalemate and then been sent running back into the sunlight—and they would’ve considered it a successful raid.  This boy in blue, however, took down groups seeking to outnumber, outflank, or surround him as though walking through a pile of leaves.  Ixë, after seeing the boy in blue pin the Blood King to the wall—burning him to a crisp that flaked away with a rasping cry that matched that of his cruel laugh—retreated to his cove, waiting for his time, ready to be released from the boredom and slow torture he embraced as his life, every day exactly the same.  But not today, he thought in a mixture of horror and giddiness.

When the boy entered Ixë’s small cove, he sized up his prey.  So too, Ixë gazed upon his doom: more of a young man than a boy, upon closer inspection…  Unwinded, with veins showing under his light skin, and perhaps aged twenty years or slightly more—he was close what would be considered prime feeding.  Ixë’s resignation dripped with this irony: here he, a vampire, stood cornered—the ideal prey.  “It is fitting for one such as I,” or at least he thought so.

The Boy in Blue held up his starlight, piercing Ixë’s sight, with a aura of peace and an unbearably bright light, arresting any thought or ability of movement in Ixë.  To his great surprise, he heard a kind voice—such as he had not heard since before he could remember: “What is your name?”

Ixë paused, intrigued, bemused even. “They call me Ixë,” he said, knowing full well only he himself had ever uttered that name.

Inspecting him up and down, the Boy in Blue remarked, “The Corruption has not fully set in. Yet there are still signs.”  Abruptly he asked, “Why did you not fight?”

Not knowing the correct answer, or perhaps only caring for the novelty of it all, he simply said, “What would be the point?”

“Perhaps you might escape,” said the Boy in Blue, seeming to nudge his guest into action.  Ixë wondered if this was a hint the blue cloaked youth knew he had originally planned to do just that.

“The surest escape would be for you to finish your work, my lad,” said Ixë with a neutral smile touching his lips.

The Boy in Blue dimmed the light ever so gently, and asked without a drop of sarcasm, “how would you like an adventure?”

Ixë blinked in response.  He turned the question this way and that, wondering what this could possibly mean, if it were true.  Releasing a slight sigh, he said, “there are no more adventures for me: I’ve seen every sweetness turn dry and fade into darkness.”

The Boy in Blue persisted, “Would you like an adventure?”

Without waiting for a response, he pressed on, “Bloody persecution prevails in the land, led by the Path of Air.  My own Order’s adherents burn at the stake or drown in water, and its Elders are hanged, drawn, and quartered.  Most of its Overseers long since succumbed to the other Path, one was killed in most gruesome fashion, a handful managed to escape into impotent exile.  The true prince is lost and must be found.  Your own redemption may hang in the balance.  Now is not the time for inaction.  You are lost, but only temporarily, if you so choose.  And, to be frank, I see certain attributes in you that could be useful.  We are to save the kingdom from itself.”

“How can you trust me?” Asked Ixë.  “I might feed on you; I might feed on this true prince of yours, should we even find him.”

The Boy in Blue laughed, piercing the air that previously moved like sludge.  “No, I think not.  Because you are simply too bored: you wouldn’t want to miss out on some newness in your life.  Because you are proud: your newfound sense of adventure would turn to an unquestionable determination to see it through.  Because you are different from the others: you know this has never been your home and you are desperate never to be chased back to another like it,” he said.  “The first step is fighting your apathy, for you will make no difficult choices if you let that rob you of initiative.  Make the choice.”

Ixë pondered this appraisal, but sensed no guile in the Boy in Blue.  “You’ve led an innocent life,” he said.  “You don’t know the acts I’ve committed.”

“I know the potential acts you could do,” said the Boy in Blue.  “The path won’t be easy.  You must learn to shed your old self.  Where there was boredom, replace it with zeal for the job.  Where there was pridefulness, replace that thought of yourself with whatever task at hand needs doing, no matter what it is.  Where there was bloodlust, replace it with restraint and the peace that comes from making the right choice.”

Ixë faltered, fearing to give in to false hope, fearing to smile lest he bare his fangs, teetering on an abyss of thoughts.

“One thing else is required, for this to work,” said the Boy in Blue.  “I must have your obedience.”

Ixë somehow knew this was real, a chance for a new beginning, but soundly unknown.  He hesitated for a moment, then merely held out his hand.  Without pause or a flicker of guardedness, the Boy in Blue grasped it with his own starlit hand, burning an image into the daywalker's palm.

“My name is Eyrad,” he declared, “and, Ixë, I henceforth name you Littlejon, a name to be shared only with the members of the Order.”

“And what Order is that?” Asked Ixë, surprised to find he still breathed.

“The Path of Light.”

