He did not hold out his hand.

Eyrad looked at it a while, the hand that stayed at Ixë’s side, as though it were a word Ixë had not yet decided to say. Then he lowered the starlight until it was no brighter than a candle, and the cove went back to being a cove.

“Then live,” he said. “For one more day, at least. I will wait at the crossroads below Thornbury until tomorrow’s sundown. Listen for the bells.”

He left the way he had come, and Ixë heard him go: through the den, past the ash that had been the Blood King, out into the light. Nobody followed him. Nobody was left to.

The next day was exactly the same. That was the horror of it.

Ixë woke in his cove. He walked the path between the trees at noon, unseen. It was hot. There were no children, because there were no children left who would play near a mountain that had screamed at them. He found a rabbit, lulled it, wrung its neck, and fed, and was still weak, and turned for home. The den was silent now. No one to avoid. No one to be beaten by. He sat in it until the light outside went orange, and understood, slowly and then all at once, that it would be this day forever: this exact day, and nothing he did inside it would ever be new again.

“You were right, boy,” he said to the empty den. “I am too bored.”

He heard the Thornbury bells before he saw the crossroads, ringing vespers through the dusk. Eyrad sat on a milestone with his cloak around his knees, and did not look surprised.

This time Ixë held out his hand first. The starlight burned the image into his palm, and it hurt exactly as much as he deserved.

“Littlejon,” said Eyrad, as if he had known the name all along. “Come on. We have a long way to walk.”

