:: ch3
Chapter Three: Mother Maud

[if not widow_freed]
Goody Ennis came up out of the water the second time still singing, and the third time she did not come up singing, and they took her down off the stool and laid her on the grass, and the crowd went home to its dinner. Sister Agathe wiped her gloves on a cloth. As she passed Eyrad in the street she said to her clerk, quite clearly, “The fen-mother next. Below Hollin, past the drowned church,” and did not notice the young man in blue, because he was only a madman.

Ixë did not speak for the whole of the next day. That night, in a hedge-bottom, he heard Eyrad weep, once, very quietly, thinking no one was awake. Ixë heard everything. He said nothing about that, either.

[end]
The fens began where the road gave up. Reeds taller than a man, black water between them, and a mist that never quite lifted even at noon, so that the sun came through it brown and weak. Ixë did not like it. In that light he was neither one thing nor the other: a smear in the air, a shape a heron flinched from. He walked behind Eyrad and watched his own hands come and go.

“They’ll be a day behind us,” said Eyrad. “Maybe less.”

They found the drowned church by its bell tower, standing out of the water like a finger. The rest of the village was under the fen. Beyond it, on a hummock of dry ground, there was a hut of reeds and tar and a woman sitting in its doorway with her hands folded, as though she had been expecting guests for a long time and had begun to find it tedious.

She was very old. Her eyes were the white of cooked egg. She lifted her face as they came up the path and breathed in through her nose, slowly, the way a vintner breathes over a cup.

“Eyrad,” she said. “You’ve grown. And you’ve brought me a Daywalker. How thoughtful. I haven’t smelled one since I was a novice, and that was a very long time ago.”

“Reverend Mother,” said Eyrad, and knelt.

“Get up, boy, the ground’s wet. I’m not reverend anything now. A baron has my abbey for his hounds, and my Sisters are scattered from here to the sea. I’m mother to nothing now but frogs.” She turned her blind eyes to the place where Ixë stood. “Come here, you. Let me have a look at you.”

He went, because there was nothing in her voice that it was possible to refuse. She put out a hand, and found his face as easily as Eyrad had found his wrist, and held his jaw the way a farrier holds a horse’s. Her fingers were cold and very strong.

“They drowned me three times, you know,” said Mother Maud conversationally. “Three different Inquisitors. The water doesn’t want me. I’m too bony.” Her thumb pressed on his lip until it found the point of a fang. “Hm. Tell me, Daywalker. What was your First Blood?”

He opened his mouth to say *I don’t remember*, and found that was not true.

A garden. A wall with roses on it, white ones. A girl of fifteen or so, in a green dress, with a circlet in her hair so thin it was hardly more than a wire, laughing at something, and then not laughing. The taste of it. Centuries of rabbits and fever-sick beggars and once, God help him, a sheep, and none of it ever like that, never again. He took his face out of Mother Maud’s hand.

“A girl,” he said. “In a garden. I was new. I didn’t know what I was doing.”

“No,” said Mother Maud, “you didn’t. But you chose very well.” She sat back on her stool. “Do you know why you walk unseen in the sun, Daywalker, when your brothers burn? I’ll tell you, since the boy hasn’t. A vampire is made by his First Blood. Drink a drunkard first and you’ll be a drunkard’s devil all your days. Drink a soldier and you’ll be a soldier. And if you drink the old blood, the blood of the House of Aur that has worn the crown of this country for a thousand years, the Corruption can’t finish what it started. It gets halfway and stops. Half in the dark and half in the light. The sun won’t burn what the old blood half-saved. It only won’t look at it.”

Ixë stood very still.

“Every other blood makes you sick,” said Mother Maud, “because every other blood is the wrong blood. There’s only one kind that would feed you properly, and you’ve not tasted it since that garden.”

He turned, slowly, to Eyrad.

Eyrad met the place where his eyes would be. “You know now what I meant by *useful,*” he said. “The prince was hidden when he was a babe. We don’t know his face. We don’t know his name. But you would know his blood across a city of ten thousand, the way a hound knows a hare.”

“A hound,” said Ixë.

“A hound that chooses its master,” said Eyrad, “is a companion.”

“And a hound that chooses its supper?”

“Is why I asked for your obedience,” said Eyrad, “and not your promise.”

Mother Maud told them the rest while the fen went dark around them. Seventeen years ago, when the old king died and the Archprior became regent until an heir could be found, which he had made very sure would never happen, she had carried a baby out of the palace in a laundry basket. There had been nowhere safe in the whole country to hide the House of Aur, so she had hidden it in the one place no one would look: she had left it on the steps of the Spire of Air in Halde, on the Feast of Winds, wrapped in a band of linen embroidered with a sun and a star. The foundlings of the Spire were raised to be Inquisitors. If the prince was alive, he was one.

She gave Ixë the other half of the swaddling band, which she had kept in a tin box under her bed for seventeen years. The scent on it was so faint that a man would have said there was none. Ixë put it to his face and nearly fell.

Then she held out her hand again. “Now,” she said. “The book.”

“What book?”

“The one in your satchel that smells of white calf and dead Inquisitor. Air’s grammar. It teaches you to take a man’s breath out of him and hold it in your fist, and it is the most corrupting thing that was ever written down, and I can smell from here that you’ve read it.” She pointed at her fire. “Burn it.”

He had read it. He had read it a dozen times, in the long dead afternoons of the coven, and never quite managed any of it. He had been close, lately. Very close.

“It might be useful,” he said, “in Halde.”

“So might your teeth,” said Mother Maud.

