He took the book out of his satchel and looked at it for a long moment: white calf gone grey with handling, the dead Inquisitor’s name scraped off the flyleaf, his own pencil marks in the margins of the chapter on the breath of sleepers.

Then he put it in her fire.

It burned badly at first, the way good vellum does, curling and blackening at the edges and refusing to catch. Then it caught all at once. The diagrams went last: a man drawn in fine brown ink with arrows going in and out of his mouth, and a hand closing on the arrows.

His palm, which had been aching faintly since Thornbury, went cool.

“Good,” said Mother Maud, who could not see the fire but could hear it perfectly well. “A thing you can do is not a thing you must. Remember that in Halde, Daywalker. You’ll need it more than the book.”

The Inquisitors’ lanterns showed at the far edge of the fen before dawn. Mother Maud would not come with them. “I’ve been drowned enough,” she said. “I’ll go deeper. They never find me in the deep.” She kissed Eyrad on the forehead and did not try to kiss Ixë, which he was grateful for, and they left her sitting in her doorway with her hands folded, waiting for the next tedious guest.

