:: ch4
Chapter Four: Halde

[if book_kept]
He had not burned it. He had promised he would, and put the book back in his satchel when Mother Maud turned her head, and his palm had burned all that night like a coal held in the fist. Eyrad, walking beside him in the dark, must have seen the faint glow of it through Ixë’s fingers, the only part of him that showed. He said nothing. A lantern shows where you stand.

[end]
[if widow_freed]
The story had reached Halde before they did. *The Thornbury Miracle*: a widow lifted out of the water by unseen hands, a voice in the Sister’s ear. There were hounds at the city gates now, big grey brutes that put their noses to every cart and every coat, and two of them lifted their heads and growled at the empty air beside Eyrad as he went through. The gate-warden laughed at them and waved the madman on.

[end]
Halde stood on a hill above a river, and on the top of the hill stood the Spire of Air: a white tower thinner and taller than any building had a right to be, with harps strung between its pinnacles that sang when the wind blew, so that the whole city lived under a long, high, endless chord. Ixë had seen it once, long ago, when it was half as tall. He had thought it beautiful then. He thought so still, and disliked himself for it.

Eyrad took a room in a pilgrims’ house under the name of Brother Jory, a madman from the north who had come to see the Feast of Winds. Ixë took nothing. He walked up the hill at noon on the first day, through the crowded streets, among a thousand people who brushed past him and felt a draught.

The Spire’s cloister was open to the sky. That was its pride: a hundred novices training under the sun, in the eye of the wind, with nothing to hide. Ixë walked in through the gate behind a cart of turnips and stood among them in the full light, unseen, and smelled.

There.

It was like being struck. Like a hand closing on his heart. Through the smell of a hundred sweating boys and the turnips and the lamp oil and the hot stone, there was a thread of something that was *that garden*: the white roses, the green dress, the taste. Centuries narrowed down to a point. He found he was gripping a pillar with both hands, and the pillar was cracking.

He followed the thread across the cloister, and it led him to a boy.

Seventeen. Dark-haired, narrow, quick, with grey-green eyes and a mouth that did not smile when everyone around it smiled. The other novices called him Brother Corin, and they stood back to give him room, the way boys stand back from the one who is best at something. He was at practice. A candle stood on a post twenty feet from him, and he was making its flame lean toward him and away, toward and away, by breathing, and nothing else. The master watching him nodded and said, “Again,” and then, “Good. You’ll take your vows at midwinter. You’ll make Inquisitor before you’re twenty.”

Beyond the practice ground there was an iron cage standing in the corner of the cloister, half in shade and half in sun, and in the shady half something huddled and hissed. Ixë knew the smell of it before he saw it: Sable, who had broken three of his fingers one winter for the pleasure of hearing him make a sound. The red cloaks had taken her alive from the coven after the raid. The novices practised on her. Brother Corin finished with the candle, picked up a hand-mirror from the bench, and, without hurry or pleasure, turned a bar of noon sunlight in through the bars onto Sable’s bare foot. Sable screamed. The boy watched her scream with exactly the same face he had worn for the candle.

“Again,” said the master.

Ixë went back down the hill. He sat on the end of Eyrad’s bed in the pilgrims’ house, and told him everything, and then said: “He’s one of them. Not pretending. *One of them.* He’d burn you on a Tuesday and eat his dinner after.”

“He has been taught to,” said Eyrad, “for seventeen years. You’ve been taught things for centuries. Look how much you’ve unlearned in a week.”

“And if he can’t?”

“Then we’ll have found the prince and lost the kingdom anyway, and it won’t matter what he is.” Eyrad stood, and settled his cloak. “I have a friend inside the Spire, an archivist who keeps the old books. Tonight I’ll go to him, and find out where the novices sleep. You’ll stay here. And Littlejon,” he said, at the door. “If I am taken, you are to take the prince and go. You are not to come for me. Do you understand me?”

“And if I don’t?”

“Then you will obey me anyway,” said Eyrad, “which is the whole point of obedience.”

He went out. The room grew dark. The harps on the Spire sang the same chord they had sung all day, and Ixë sat in the dark with the thread of that blood still in his nose, and thought about Sable’s foot, and the boy’s face, and found he could not stop thinking about either.

