:: ch5
Chapter Five: Brother Corin

Eyrad did not come back.

At midnight Ixë went to look for him. Night in Halde was a different country: no sun to hide in, and he was only a thin pale man in a dark coat, quicker than he looked, who kept to the alleys. He climbed the Spire’s outer wall where the stones were rough with age and came over it into a little garden behind the archive, and there, through a lit window, he saw how it had gone.

The archivist was on his knees on the floor with his hands tied and his face wet, saying *I’m sorry, I’m sorry* to no one. And Eyrad stood in the middle of the room with his hands open at his sides, and his mouth open, and nothing coming out of it. Not words. Not breath. Not light.

A tall man in white stood in front of him with one hand raised and closed into a fist. He was old and handsome and he had the patient, considering face of a physician at a sickbed. Ixë had never seen the Archprior of Air, but he knew him, the way one knows the shape of a hawk overhead.

“The last free Elder of the Light,” said the Archprior, in a voice like a cool cloth on a forehead. “And so young. Though not as young as you were, I think. Your Light keeps its accounts in grey hairs. You can’t make light without breath, can you, boy? Nobody can. That was always the Light’s weakness: you have to say it.” He turned his closed fist a little, and Eyrad’s whole body arched. “Tomorrow, at noon, in the Court of Winds. Let the city watch the Light go out.”

He opened his hand. Eyrad dropped to his knees, dragging in air like a drowning man, and before he could shape a single word, two Inquisitors had a gag in his mouth.

Ixë went down off the wall into the dark.

*Take the prince and go. You are not to come for me.*

He found the novices’ dormitory by the smell: one long room under the roof, forty boys asleep in rows, and the thread of the garden running through all of it, clear as a bell. He followed it to the end bed. Brother Corin slept on his back with his hands folded on his chest, as he had been taught. In the dark Ixë was visible as any man. He stood over the bed for a long time.

He could feel the pulse in the boy’s throat from where he stood. He could have counted it. He did count it.

The mark on his palm began, very slowly, to glow.

Brother Corin opened his eyes.

He did not scream. He looked up at the shape standing over him, at the pale face and the faint starlight in its hand, and his breathing changed, deliberately, the way it had changed in the cloister to bend the candle. Ixë felt the pull of it on his own chest, a hook in the lungs, and it was strong, much stronger than Sister Agathe; and it slid off him all the same.

The boy’s eyes widened very slightly. That was all.

“That won’t work,” said Ixë, quietly. “Nothing like that has ever worked on me. Don’t shout. I’m not going to hurt you. If I were, I’d have done it while you slept.”

“You’re one of the things from the cage,” said Corin. His voice was low and very steady. “The master says you lie as naturally as you breathe. Except you don’t breathe.”

“I do, as it happens. I’m not a very good one.” Ixë sat on the end of the bed, which seemed to astonish the boy more than anything else had, and took out the half of the swaddling band and laid it on the blanket between them. “Have you ever seen this before?”

Corin looked at it. He did not touch it. Then, very slowly, he reached under his pillow and took out a strip of linen, yellow with age, carefully folded, embroidered at one end with half a sun and half a star. He had kept it under his pillow for seventeen years, Ixë thought, and nobody in this room knew.

The two halves lay side by side on the blanket. The sun was whole. So was the star.

“They found me on the steps,” said the boy. “At the Feast of Winds. Who are you? What is this?”

There was not much time. In the morning they would gag Eyrad and walk him out into the Court of Winds. Ixë needed this boy to get up and come with him, and quickly, and he knew already what the boy feared most in the world, because he had watched him turn a mirror onto Sable’s foot without blinking.

