He stepped back, out of the faint glow of his own palm, into the deep dark at the end of the dormitory, where nothing at all could be seen. Then he spoke, and he let his voice do the thing it had always been good at, the thing he had named himself for: he let it come from everywhere and nowhere, from the rafters and the floor and the space between the boy’s own ears.

“You were given to the wind, Corin,” said the voice. “Now the wind has come for you.”

The boy’s breath caught. Then he did what he had been trained to do, every day of his life, when the wind spoke: he got out of bed and knelt on the floorboards and bowed his head.

Ixë’s palm burned as if he had closed his fist on a coal. He put the hand behind his back.

“Your name is not Corin,” said the voice. “Tomorrow at noon there will be a burning in the Court of Winds. You will go to it, as all novices must. When I say *now*, you will walk with me out of the Gate of Breezes, and you will not look back. Will you do this?”

“Yes,” whispered the boy. He was shaking. His face, turned up to the dark, was alight with a joy so complete and so simple that Ixë had to look away from it. “Yes. Anything. I always knew. I always knew you’d come.”

It would have been easier, Ixë thought, going back down the wall in the cold before dawn, if the boy had screamed.

