:: ch6
Chapter Six: Noon

[if not lied]
He had told the boy everything, sitting on the end of the bed in the dark: what he was, and what Corin was, and the garden, and the girl in green, whose great-great-something grandson was now sitting very straight in a novice’s bed with his knuckles white on two halves of a swaddling band. The boy had listened to all of it without a sound. At the end, he had said: “I could call the master. I could shout. Forty Inquisitors would be in this room before you got to the window.” And Ixë had said, “Yes,” and waited. And the boy had not shouted. He had not said he believed it, either. He had only said: “Noon, then,” and turned his face to the wall.

[end]
The Court of Winds was the great square below the Spire, paved in white stone that threw the sun back into everyone’s eyes, and by noon on the Feast of Winds the whole of Halde was packed into it shoulder to shoulder. The harps on the Spire were singing. There was a stake in the middle of the square with faggots piled round it to the height of a man’s waist, and a ring of red cloaks around that, and a platform of white wood where the Archprior stood with his hands folded in his sleeves.

The novices of the Spire stood in rows before the platform, as was required. Brother Corin stood in the second row. Ixë stood beside him, unseen in the full blaze of noon, close enough that their sleeves touched. The boy felt it, and did not flinch.

They brought Eyrad out.

He walked between two Inquisitors with his hands bound behind him and the gag in his mouth, and his blue cloak had been taken from him and he wore a shift of undyed wool, and his feet were bare on the burning stone. He did not look at the crowd. He did not look at the stake. He looked, once, at the rows of novices, and his eyes passed over the place where Ixë stood as though there were nothing there at all, which there was not, and then they came back, and rested there for one moment, and Ixë understood that Eyrad knew exactly where he was.

Then he looked away, toward the Gate of Breezes at the far side of the square, where the crowd was thinnest. And back. And away again.

*Go.*

The Archprior began to speak. His voice took the breath of the whole square in its hand and squeezed, gently, so that ten thousand people swayed and sighed together, and the harps sang over all of it.

“Now,” said Ixë in Corin’s ear. “Walk with me. Don’t look back.”

The Gate of Breezes was two hundred paces away across the white stone. Behind them two Inquisitors were tying Eyrad to the stake. Ixë had a mace on his belt and a bow on his back and the sun on every inch of him, and nobody in that square could see him, and he could walk up behind the Archprior on his platform and do anything at all.

