“Stay here,” he said to Corin. “Exactly here. Don’t move until I say.”

He walked out across the white stone toward the stake, and nobody saw him go.

The Inquisitors were piling the last of the faggots. He stepped between them as if between trees, and drew his knife, and cut the ropes at Eyrad’s wrists, and then the gag; Eyrad’s eyes went wide, and Ixë said in his ear, “Breathe,” and he did, a great tearing gasp of air, and the Archprior on his platform turned his head sharply toward the stake, like a man who hears a coin fall.

[if book_kept]
The Archprior raised his fist to take Eyrad’s breath again, and Ixë did the thing from the book, the thing he had almost managed a dozen times in the coven and never quite: he reached out across fifty feet of empty air and closed his own hand on the Archprior’s breath instead. It was like gripping a live eel. It was horrible. The tall man in white made a small, surprised noise, and staggered, and for one moment, one long moment, he could not breathe at all.

[else]
The Archprior raised his fist to take Eyrad’s breath again, and Ixë did the only thing he had, the only thing he had ever had: he screamed. The scream came out of nowhere, out of the white stone itself, out of ten thousand throats at once, the same bloodcurdling scream that had sent the children running home down the path below the mountain a lifetime ago, and the Archprior flinched, and lost his grip, for one moment.

[end]
One moment was enough.

Eyrad stood up out of the faggots, and opened his hands, and spoke.

Ixë never afterwards remembered the word. He remembered Eyrad’s face as he said it, close enough to touch: the skin at his eyes creasing, grey running through his brown hair like frost through grass, ten years going out of him in a breath. And he remembered the light. It came out of Eyrad like a second sun, white and blue and silent, and the Court of Winds went blind, all ten thousand of them at once, and the harps on the Spire screamed and snapped their strings, and the Archprior went down on his platform with his arms over his face.

“Fool,” said Eyrad, very quietly, somewhere in all that brilliance. His voice was hoarse and he was smiling. “Go. Take him. I’ll hold the gate.” And Ixë felt a hand on his shoulder, pushing him toward the Gate of Breezes, and then the hand was gone, and so was Eyrad, and there was only light.

He found Corin exactly where he had left him, blind and terrified and not moving, because he had been told not to.

