:: ch7
Chapter Seven: Shade

They went out by the Gate of Breezes and down to the river and along it, westward, and behind them Halde was ringing like a struck bell.

[if not saved_eyrad]
Corin had looked back, once, at the gate. Ixë had not been able to stop him. Over the heads of the crowd, where the stake stood, there had been a column of smoke going up, and then, inside the smoke, a light: a single point of white and blue, brighter than noon, rising. The crowd’s noise had changed. Then the gate was behind them and they could not see anything at all. “Is he dead?” the boy had asked, and Ixë had said, “I don’t know,” which was the truth, and the hardest thing he had said in centuries.

[end]
By evening they were in the Weald, the old forest that runs along the river for fifty miles, and the hounds were behind them.

He had known it would come to the trees, and he had not let himself think about it. Under the canopy, in the green afternoon shade, he came back into the world: first a shimmer, then a shape, then a thin pale man in a dark coat, with dark hair and a pointed face and, when he forgot to keep his mouth closed, the points of his teeth. He heard Corin stop walking behind him.

[if lied]
“You’re not a spirit,” said the boy. His voice was very small. “You’re not a spirit. You’re one of the things from the cage.”

Ixë turned to face him. There was no voice from everywhere now. There was just his own, coming out of his own mouth, which was the only place it had ever really come from.

“Yes,” he said.

Corin looked at him for a long time. He had a knife in his belt, a novice’s knife, and his hand was on it. “You lied to me.”

“Yes.”

“Why?”

“Because I thought you wouldn’t come with a monster,” said Ixë. “And because I am one, and that’s what we do.” He held out his hand, palm up, and the star in it was glowing, hot and bright and angry, the way it had glowed all night. “This burns when I lie. It hasn’t stopped burning since your dormitory. You can have that, if it helps.”

The boy took his hand off the knife. He did not say anything. But he started walking again.

[else]
Corin looked at him in the green light, properly, for the first time: the face he had been told about in the dark, the teeth. Then he looked down at the burn-scar of the star in Ixë’s palm, and back up, and said, “I thought you’d be taller,” and kept walking.

[end]
The first arrow came out of the trees at dusk. It took Ixë high in the back, under the shoulder blade, and it was not an ordinary arrow; its head was hammered silver and its shaft was wrapped in a strip of white calf written over with the grammar of breath, and when it went in, all the air went out of him and did not come back. He went down on his knees in the leaf-mould. Corin pulled him up and dragged him on, and they ran, if it could be called running, until the hounds’ voices bent away eastward after some other scent, and then they fell into a hollow under the roots of a beech and lay there.

Corin cut the arrow out with his novice’s knife. He was good at it. The Spire taught its novices to dig out arrowheads and splinters and worse, for the Inquisitors who came home from raids on the covens with something still in them. The wound did not close. It would not, Ixë knew, without blood. He had not fed since a hare in a beech wood south of Thornbury, a hundred years ago, last week. He tried to call something to him in the dark, a rabbit, a fox, and found the forest empty for a mile around: the hounds had frightened everything into its hole.

He was going grey at the edges. He could feel it. In the morning the red cloaks would come back with the hounds, and he would be lying here in the shade where anyone could see him, and the boy with him.

“Go on,” he said. “West, along the river. Keep the sun on your left in the morning. There’s a ferry at Ashby. Tell the ferryman you’re a pilgrim from the north.”

Corin did not go. He sat with his back against the roots and his knife on his knees and looked at Ixë in the dark for a long time.

“You could have let them have me,” he said at last. “Last night. You could have had me yourself. You stood over my bed for long enough. I was awake. I heard you counting.”

Ixë said nothing.

The boy turned his left hand over and, before Ixë could stop him, drew the knife across the heel of it, a short shallow cut, and held it out. In the dark, the smell of it was the garden. The white roses. The green dress. The girl laughing. All of it, centuries of it, in one hand an inch from his mouth.

“Take it,” said the Prince of the House of Aur. “Only what you need. I’ll know if it’s more.”

