:: ch8
Chapter Eight: The Path of Light

He drank only what was given.

It was the garden. It was exactly the garden, the taste he had chased for centuries, through rabbits and beggars and once a sheep. And it was not the garden at all, because the girl in the green dress had not held her hand out to him. She had screamed. This boy was holding his hand out. Ixë found that the difference between those two things was the difference between everything and nothing, and that he had not known it until now, and that he was crying, which vampires do not do, and which the boy was polite enough not to mention.

He stopped. It was the hardest thing he had ever done, harder than standing in the Court of Winds, harder than the millpond. He stopped, and took his mouth away, and closed the boy’s hand over the cut, and held it closed.

The wound in his back closed. The star in his palm, which had glowed all through it, went quiet and silver and cool.

“Thank you,” said Corin, strangely, as if Ixë had given him something.

They could not go west in the morning. The red cloaks were on the river road, with Sister Agathe of Thornbury riding at their head; Ixë heard her voice across a mile of water, and knew it. They could not go east, where Halde was. There was only one direction left, and he knew by noon where it led, because he knew the shape of the hills: north, into the foothills, up the path between the trees where there was no shade at all, to the small mountain where he had lived for longer than he cared to remember.

“There,” he said. “They won’t follow us in. Not with the sun going down. Nobody goes into a coven at night.”

“Isn’t it empty?” said Corin. “Wasn’t it raided?”

“Nothing that size is ever empty,” said Ixë.

It was not. The survivors of the raid had come back to it, as he had known they would: a dozen of them, the ones who had been out hunting when Eyrad came, the ones who had hidden in the deep tunnels. And on the stone seat in the den where the old Blood King had sat sat a new one: Mordrecht, who had held Ixë down that winter while Sable broke his fingers, and laughed.

“Daywalker,” said Mordrecht, delighted, from his throne, as they came in out of the last of the light. “And you’ve brought us dinner.”

“He isn’t dinner,” said Ixë. “And there are twenty red cloaks a mile behind me who are. With hounds, and an Inquisitor Sister who would make a very good supper for a king. All yours. If you let us through to the high vent.”

Mordrecht considered this, smiling. Then he stopped smiling.

[if book_kept]
Ixë had closed his hand. Across the den, on his throne, Mordrecht’s mouth opened and nothing came out of it, not words, not breath; Ixë held the breath of the Blood King in his fist like a live eel, and it was horrible, and he did not let go until Mordrecht nodded.

[else]
“No,” said Mordrecht. “I think we’ll have the boy now, and the red cloaks after. And you’ll watch, Daywalker, the way you always used to.”

Around the den, in the dark, a dozen shapes stood up.

Ixë had a mace on his belt and a bow on his back, and Corin’s blood was still warm in him, and it was not enough. It had never been enough, not in this room. He had read Air’s grammar a dozen times and could have stopped a heart with it, and he had burned it in an old woman’s fire because she asked him to. He thought, very clearly, *that was the right thing to do*, and then, *and now I have to find another way.*

His left palm was aching. He looked down at it: the star, quiet and silver since the beech wood, the only light in the den.

[end]
