He held up his hand.

“You saw what came into this den a month ago,” he said. “A boy in blue, with a light in his hand. You saw what he did to the old King, there, against that wall.” He turned the palm so they could all see it. “He put this on me so he could find me. He is an hour behind us on the mountain. If the prince doesn’t walk out of here, he’ll come in after him, and none of you will be here to tell the next King how it went.”

It was a lie. Eyrad was ash, or a star, or a man somewhere very far away; Ixë did not know which. And the mark knew it was a lie. It burned as it had burned all the night he kept the book, white and hot and terrible, so that the light came out between his fingers and lit the whole den, every face in it, and up into the dark of the roof.

It was the only light those faces had seen in that den since the Boy in Blue. They had watched it burn their King to a flake.

Mordrecht looked at it for a long time. Then he nodded, once, and the shapes in the dark sat down.

The light went on burning in Ixë’s fist all the way up the tunnel, and he let it. It shows you where you stand.

