:: ch8_tunnel
They waited in the dark of the high tunnel while the Inquisitors came up the mountain. Ixë could hear them: the hounds first, then the boots, then Sister Agathe’s voice telling them to light the torches, that there was nothing in a raided coven but ash and a frightened Daywalker. They came into the den with their torches held high and their breath held tight, twenty of them, and the coven waited in the dark around them, and did not move, and Ixë could hear every one of them swallowing.

He could let it happen. He had promised it. It was a fair trade, by the only law the mountain knew: the widow at Thornbury for the woman who had held her under, Sable’s foot for the boys who held the mirror, and all of them the coven’s, and the boy safe. Nobody would blame him. Eyrad was not here to see.

Corin’s hand found his sleeve in the dark. It did not grip. It rested.

“Littlejon,” the boy said. It was the first time he had used the name; Ixë did not know when he had learned it. “No one dies in the dark for me. That’s my first order. I think I’m allowed to give them now.”

In the den below, Sister Agathe raised her torch, and the coven began, very softly, to move.

