:: ending_littlejon [finale]
(((
The canon ending. The platform shows the rating box after any finale.
)))
He went down into the den with his hands empty and walked into the middle of the torches.

The Inquisitors saw him, because there was no sun in a coven: a thin pale man with his teeth showing. Twenty bows came up. Sister Agathe’s voice took the breath of the den in its fist, and it slid off him.

“There are twelve vampires in this room,” said Ixë, “and you can see one of them. Put out your torches and you’ll see none. Follow me, now, quietly, and every one of you will see the sun come up.”

Nobody moved. Then the boy came down out of the dark behind him, a novice of the Spire in a torn grey habit, with a cut on his hand, and stood at Ixë’s side, and Sister Agathe looked at his face for a long moment in the torchlight. Every Inquisitor in that den had seen it before. It was on the old coins. It was on the windows of the palace that nobody was allowed to go into any more.

She lowered her staff.

He led them up through the high tunnels, twenty red cloaks and a Sister and a prince, in single file, in the dark, past things that breathed and did not move because Mordrecht had nodded, and they came out on the shoulder of the mountain just as the sky in the east went grey. When the sun came over the hills, the Inquisitors saw their guide fade into the light, in front of their eyes, until there was nothing there but a voice.

“Go home,” said the voice. “Tell your Archprior the House of Aur sends its regards.”

Eyrad was waiting at the foot of the path. His feet were wrapped in rags and his starlight was no brighter than a candle, but it was there. So was the grey: through all of his hair now, not only at the temples, and his face had the lines of a man of forty. Whatever he had done in the Court of Winds, he had paid for it in years.

[if saved_eyrad]
“You disobeyed me,” he said, as Ixë came down the path, unseen. “And you were right to. Once. Don’t make a habit of it.”

[else]
“You obeyed,” he said, as Ixë came down the path, unseen. “It was the hardest thing I’ll ever ask of you. I am sorry I had to.”

[end]
The prince of the House of Aur came down behind him, and stopped, and looked at Eyrad, and at the empty air beside him. “He says his name’s Ixë,” said the boy. “And you call him Littlejon.”

“I do,” said Eyrad.

“Which is it?”

Ixë thought about it, standing in the sun on the path where he had once frightened children for something to do. It was a hot morning. There was no shade on the path at all. It was exactly the same path as on a hundred thousand mornings before, and it was not the same at all, because nothing ever had been, it turned out, not really; he had only stopped looking.

“Both,” he said. “Come on. We have a long way to walk.”

:: ending_den [finale]
(((
The fight, for a Linker who burned the book and chose to fight anyway.
)))
He went for Mordrecht across the den, and for one moment, one bright moment, he was the thing the Boy in Blue had seen in him: quick and furious and certain.

Then Mordrecht caught him by the throat, the way he always had.

It did not take long. It never had, in that room. The last thing Ixë heard, because he always heard everything, was the boy’s voice, not screaming, saying his name, the secret one, *Littlejon*, over and over, as the dark closed in around him from every side; and then, far off down the mountain, hounds, and torches, and Sister Agathe telling someone very calmly that the novice was to be taken back to the Spire alive.

Every day after that was exactly the same, for the boy, in a white room at the top of a white tower, until the day there were no more of them. Nobody who came after him knew his name, either of them.

:: ending_blood_king [finale]
(((
The fork ending from `bloodlust`. The Corruption finishes what it started.
)))
He did not stop.

The boy said *enough*, once, very quietly, and then he did not say anything. It was the garden. It was the whole garden, the white roses and the green dress and centuries of thirst, and it did not stop, it went on and on, and the star in Ixë’s palm burned white, and then red, and then went out, and the scar peeled away from his skin like the paper off an old letter and blew away in the dark.

When it was over he sat back on his heels under the beech tree and felt the Corruption finish its work.

It did not hurt. That was the surprise of it. It felt like coming home. His back closed. His strength came into him like wine, the full strength of a vampire who has had his fill of the one blood that was made for him: enough, he understood, to tear the throat out of a hound with his hands, to put a whole square of men to sleep with his voice, to stand in front of a Boy in Blue and not kneel.

In the morning he stood up and walked out of the trees into the sun, and the sun saw him for the first time in centuries, and began to burn.

He walked back into the shade, and smiled, and found that he did not mind.

He went north that night, up the path between the trees, to the small mountain. He came into the den out of the dark, and Mordrecht looked up from the stone seat and said *Daywalker* in a voice that was already afraid, and Ixë took the seat from him, and Mordrecht with it.

Every day after that was exactly the same. That was the one thing he had wanted, he told himself, sitting on the stone seat in the dark, while the coven brought him what he needed. And this time, every day was his.

:: ending_echo [finale]
(((
The fork ending from `let_the_coven_feed`. He keeps his bargain, and
loses the boy.
)))
He did nothing.

It took a very short time. The torches went out first, all at once, and after that he did not watch; he only heard, and he heard everything, because he always had. Sister Agathe’s voice was the last to stop.

When it was over, Mordrecht let them through to the high vent, as he had promised, and they climbed out onto the shoulder of the mountain just as the sky in the east went grey.

The boy did not speak all the way up. At the top he sat down on a rock with his cut hand in his lap and looked at the sunrise, and then, at last, at the thin pale man beside him, who was beginning to fade.

“It was fair,” said Ixë. “By the law of the mountain, it was fair. You don’t know what she did at Thornbury.”

“I don’t need to,” said the boy. “I know it was fair.” He stood up. “I’ll find my way to Eyrad. You’ll go now. Don’t follow me.”

“That isn’t yours to order.”

“No,” said the Prince of the House of Aur. “It’s yours.”

Ixë went. The sun came up, and he walked out into it, unseen, down the path between the trees, where there was no shade at all.

“What a hot day,” he said to himself, at noon, in the middle of summer, and nobody answered him, because nobody ever had, except once.
